WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.416
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.770
SAMRAT RAY: Hello, everyone.

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:12.900
Welcome to this talk
about best practices

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.660
that we have learned in
Google production security

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:19.350
and how we can apply
them with Istio.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:22.432
So, the focus for today's--

00:00:22.432 --> 00:00:24.660
oh, sorry.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:26.460
One of the focus for today--

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:31.140
we'll talk about how the
production landscape is

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:35.040
changing, what is its
impact on network security,

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.800
and why Google's approach
is actually increasingly

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.980
relevant and now more
approachable than ever

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:43.230
for the enterprise.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:46.950
And Istio is a means
to an end of achieving

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.210
a security of purpose, right?

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:51.912
The key thing is to implement a
set of key security principles

00:00:51.912 --> 00:00:53.370
that we have learned
over the years

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:56.550
to protect our microservice's
infrastructures within Google's

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:57.640
production.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.540
And that's what we
will talk about.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.040
And I am Samrat Ray.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:05.190
I'm a Product Manager on Google
Cloud's Security and Privacy

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:06.510
organization.

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:09.870
And my focus is on service
security and insider threat.

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:12.300
With me is Julien Boeuf.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.180
He is a Network Security
Lead for Google's production.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:17.268
And I think this session
is special in the sense

00:01:17.268 --> 00:01:19.560
that you get to hear from
the people who actually build

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.770
this stuff, which is Julien,
right, versus the people who

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:25.360
generally talk about it.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:30.680
So, with that, without
further delays,

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:33.720
I'll hand it over
to Julien to start

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:38.040
explaining how he sees the tech
model evolve over the changing

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:41.757
production needs, and
then talk about how

00:01:41.757 --> 00:01:43.590
the principles of Google
production security

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:46.972
that have informed our posture
for over the last decade or so.

00:01:46.972 --> 00:01:49.180
JULIEN BOEUF: All right,
thank you very much, Samrat.

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:52.350
And hi, everyone.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:56.290
So, production infrastructure
is changing, right?

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:59.560
And that has a big
impact on security.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:04.980
So, we are observing
a number of trends

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.510
from our customers
and our partners.

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:10.139
And there are, like,
three major trends

00:02:10.139 --> 00:02:11.940
that I want to talk about.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:17.820
The first trend is the use
of orchestration frameworks

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:20.110
and containers.

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:23.280
And the most popular of these
orchestration frameworks

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.650
is Kubernetes today.

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:31.540
The second trend is the use
of hybrid and multi-cloud,

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:34.710
meaning that one workload may
be in one environment on-premise

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.950
and can move to another
environment on the cloud

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:39.180
provider.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.172
And the third trend is the
adoption of microservices.

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:50.980
So let's start with
containers and hybrid.

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:53.270
And I will have--
a big claim here

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:57.540
is that IP-based network
security is suboptimal.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:01.440
So your IP tables
can only go so far.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.330
And why is that?

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:06.080
That's because when
you think about it,

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:10.070
an orchestration framework is
going to schedule containers

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:12.770
on a pool of hosts, right?

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:19.040
And the orchestration
framework can kill the workload

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.920
on the host, reschedule
that on another host,

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:28.580
and as a consequence, the IP
and the host IP in particular

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:32.270
doesn't have a lot of
relevance anymore, right?

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:36.500
These containers, also these
workloads-- they're ephemeral.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:38.600
They can be started and
stopped very quickly.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:43.600
Now for hybrid, as
I said, you will

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:48.910
have a workload that starts
on an environment on-premise.

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:52.720
And then that moves to another
environment which is cloud.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.348
And when that occurs,
you don't want

00:03:55.348 --> 00:03:56.890
to have to reprogram
all your network

00:03:56.890 --> 00:04:00.280
policies because the subnet
is not the same, right?

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:03.340
And so what that
means is that we

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:06.400
need policies and access logs.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:08.410
Like, policies and
access logs-- they

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:11.560
need to have a stable
identifier for the workload.

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:19.060
The other trend
is microservices.

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:26.500
And when you think about your
old monolithic application

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:33.850
where everything was
integrated in one big binary,

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:36.280
that monolith got exploded
into a constellation

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:37.690
of microservices.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:39.850
And as a consequence, calls--

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:42.850
a function call that
used to be local--

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:46.570
are now completely
distributed over the network.

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:49.690
And what that means in
terms of network security

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:55.570
is that your network is
much more privileged now

00:04:55.570 --> 00:04:59.410
if you don't do anything about
it, meaning that somebody who

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:02.230
has a privileged
stance on the network

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:06.280
is able to do-- be able to do
much more damage than before.

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:09.320
So we need a solution for that.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:12.820
The other point that I want
to make around microservices

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:17.060
is that they expose a
lot of different methods.

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:20.020
And let's take an example of
a key distribution service,

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:22.570
for example.

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:24.340
This key distribution
service is going

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:27.370
to expose the methods that
have very different security

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:28.630
properties.

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:31.540
One, method call can be
completely innocuous,

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:33.250
such as a health check.

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:37.390
You want to check whether
the service is dead or alive.

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:40.120
And you will have another
call that is going

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:43.640
to be rotate the master key.

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:47.680
And this one needs to be
really heavily protected.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.170
And again, what this
means is that the policies

00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:54.230
need to be able to reason
about these different methods

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:57.750
and the different
levels of privilege.

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:00.500
So that means that the
policies have to go way

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:02.710
beyond layer 3 and layer 4.

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:04.490
They have to operate at layer 7.

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:12.010
So the good news
here to some extent

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:15.970
is that adoption of
containerized workloads

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:18.040
and microservices-- that's
what Google has been

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:19.910
doing for the last 10 years.

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:22.390
We've been doing
that over a decade.

00:06:22.390 --> 00:06:24.970
And we have in
production millions

00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:28.390
of microservices endpoints.

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:33.070
And so we're informing our
network security posture

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:34.478
by a set of principles.

00:06:34.478 --> 00:06:36.520
And I'm going to talk
about these principles now.

00:06:41.670 --> 00:06:44.550
So, I'm going to start
this part of the talk

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:48.420
by a quote from Niels Provos.

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:52.560
Niels used to be our
security horizontal lead.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:58.500
And he gave a talk on production
security in this very forum

00:06:58.500 --> 00:06:59.830
a couple of years ago.

00:06:59.830 --> 00:07:02.520
And at the time, he
said, "At Google,

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:06.540
we are not pursuing a
hardshell perimeter model.

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:13.030
Instead, we're really focused
on pervasive defense in depth."

00:07:13.030 --> 00:07:14.470
There's another
term that you may

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:18.370
have heard about pervasive
defense in depth.

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:21.240
And it's zero-trust security.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:24.910
So personally, I'm not a big
fan of zero-trust security

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:27.120
because at the end
of the day, you

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:31.750
have to trust something,
or a set of things, right?

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:34.190
If you go with an
identity-based system,

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:36.970
you will have to trust
your identity provider,

00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:39.232
your identity issuing system.

00:07:39.232 --> 00:07:40.690
You will have to
trust your network

00:07:40.690 --> 00:07:43.550
for availability in any case.

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:46.180
So I prefer the term "defense
in depth," which I think

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:48.070
is more correct.

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:54.960
And you can see defense in
depth along a set of dimensions.

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:59.890
And a first dimension may
be how to build your stock

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:03.910
on a production machine from
the hardware [INAUDIBLE] trust

00:08:03.910 --> 00:08:09.820
to a bootloader to an
operating system to containers

00:08:09.820 --> 00:08:11.620
and applications.

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:15.790
And how do you make sure
that, at each layer,

00:08:15.790 --> 00:08:19.200
what you're running is
what you think is running

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:20.740
that's not malicious code?

00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:23.800
And that's one type--

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:26.380
one dimension of
defense in depth.

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:28.180
The other type of
defense in depth

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:32.080
that we can think about
is along the network path

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:36.080
and how you can have
two layers of security--

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:39.710
one that is at the
edge of the perimeter,

00:08:39.710 --> 00:08:42.250
or at the edge of the production
network, and the other that

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:45.850
is inside, within the
production network.

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:49.300
And since we don't have
that much time today,

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:52.280
we're going to focus on the
second one, the network path.

00:08:57.140 --> 00:08:59.100
So, the first
principle that we have

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:01.530
is that we need two layers.

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:05.130
So the first layer is that you
have to protect your production

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:07.530
network at the edge, right?

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:14.250
And proxies are the thing
that provide this isolation.

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:19.490
You can see on this
diagram here you have--

00:09:19.490 --> 00:09:21.510
our customer is the blue.

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:25.050
And the yellow person as
going through the Google front

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:30.360
end in order to invoke
a service at Google.

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:32.280
This Google front
end is going to do

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:35.180
a bunch of security checks.

00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:38.120
And you can see that if an
attacker tries to attack us

00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:42.500
here with, for example, a
denial-of-service attack,

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:46.430
we can detect this attack and
we can react appropriately

00:09:46.430 --> 00:09:50.810
through aggressive rate
limiting, for example.

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:54.380
Now for workloads that
are within the network,

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:58.430
we ensure that these workloads
cannot arbitrarily egress out

00:09:58.430 --> 00:10:03.770
to the internet because that
would be an obvious vector

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:05.880
for exfiltration of data.

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:10.420
So we have perimeter
security here as well.

00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:14.330
And even within our
corporate network,

00:10:14.330 --> 00:10:17.170
we make sure that in order to
access the production network,

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:21.420
we go through an
administrative proxy.

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:26.610
And you may have heard of
BeyondCorp, which is something

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:29.670
that we are very keen on.

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:32.580
But there are sometimes
some misconceptions

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:34.750
about what BeyondCorp is about.

00:10:34.750 --> 00:10:39.660
So, BeyondCorp is
about the fact that

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:43.350
being on the corporate
network per se doesn't just

00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:45.353
give you access to the
production network.

00:10:45.353 --> 00:10:46.770
There are a bunch
of other signals

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:50.430
that you need to take
into account in order

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:53.700
for you to have access to
some privileged resources

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:55.980
within the production network.

00:10:55.980 --> 00:11:02.040
And the things are,
for example, what

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:06.210
is the device from which
you're operating this request

00:11:06.210 --> 00:11:08.250
to the production network?

00:11:08.250 --> 00:11:09.540
Is it a trusted device?

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:11.220
Is it an untrusted device?

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:14.160
Who is the person who is
authenticated on this device?

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:16.410
Is it a Google employee?

00:11:16.410 --> 00:11:18.120
Is it something else, right?

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:23.670
And the admin proxy
is the one that will,

00:11:23.670 --> 00:11:27.420
based on all the signal and the
type of resource that you want

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:28.920
to access in the
production network,

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:33.090
make the decision as to whether
you can or cannot access

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:34.290
the resource in question.

00:11:39.010 --> 00:11:40.810
But the thing that I
want to tell you here

00:11:40.810 --> 00:11:43.360
is that you don't
stop there, right?

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:45.370
You don't stop at the perimeter.

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:47.290
Within the production
network, you

00:11:47.290 --> 00:11:49.900
have to protect your
workloads for all the reasons

00:11:49.900 --> 00:11:53.110
that we talked about before
with respect to microservices,

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:53.770
for example.

00:11:58.780 --> 00:12:00.970
So how do we do that?

00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:03.630
The first thing that
we do is that we

00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:07.620
use credentials-backed
identities for all the entities

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:08.850
on the network.

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:14.520
And the entities are the
administrators, the machines,

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:17.410
and the workloads.

00:12:17.410 --> 00:12:19.870
And let me talk
to you about ALTS

00:12:19.870 --> 00:12:24.740
because ALTS is Application
Layer Transport Security.

00:12:24.740 --> 00:12:27.340
It's also known as loas.

00:12:27.340 --> 00:12:31.580
And that is the system that we
use to do this type of thing.

00:12:31.580 --> 00:12:36.350
So, ALTS does two main things.

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:40.900
The first thing that it does
is that it provisions, rotates,

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:45.190
and revokes all the
credentials for all

00:12:45.190 --> 00:12:46.620
these different entities.

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:48.370
That's the first
thing that it does.

00:12:48.370 --> 00:12:52.910
But ALTS is also a
transport security protocol,

00:12:52.910 --> 00:12:57.760
meaning that it's extremely
similar to mutual TLS

00:12:57.760 --> 00:13:01.590
and it's certificate-based
and private-key-based.

00:13:01.590 --> 00:13:08.250
So now let's see how Alice, who
is a site reliability engineer,

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:13.050
can schedule a workload,
which is Doodle front

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:17.953
end on the production machine.

00:13:17.953 --> 00:13:19.370
So the first thing
that Alice does

00:13:19.370 --> 00:13:24.440
is that Alice makes a request
to the Borg Master, which

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:29.750
is our scheduler, or our main
scheduler, for workloads.

00:13:29.750 --> 00:13:34.830
And she gives a description of
the Doodle front end workload.

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:37.470
And she also says,
I want this workload

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:41.470
to run as the Doodle
front end identity.

00:13:41.470 --> 00:13:44.720
And she does that over on
ALTS, which will reauthenticate

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:47.540
the channel.

00:13:47.540 --> 00:13:50.330
Once the Borg Master
receives this request,

00:13:50.330 --> 00:13:53.390
the Borg Master is going
to do two types of checks.

00:13:53.390 --> 00:13:56.900
The first check is
going to be, does Alice

00:13:56.900 --> 00:14:01.660
have the right to start a
workload as the Doodle front

00:14:01.660 --> 00:14:03.830
end identity?

00:14:03.830 --> 00:14:05.910
The first thing, right?

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:08.970
Can she act as this identity?

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:12.570
And the second check that is
going to be done is that, oh,

00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:15.990
but this workload comes with a
configuration about the binary

00:14:15.990 --> 00:14:18.660
that I'm going to be running.

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:21.390
And do we have some information
about the provenance

00:14:21.390 --> 00:14:23.420
of this binary?

00:14:23.420 --> 00:14:27.440
And that's called
binary authorization.

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:29.570
Once these two
checks are cleared,

00:14:29.570 --> 00:14:33.610
the Borg Master will then
choose a production machine,

00:14:33.610 --> 00:14:38.300
a worker machine on
yellow here, and will,

00:14:38.300 --> 00:14:41.300
through an ALTS channel
again-- that is what we have

00:14:41.300 --> 00:14:43.220
authenticated and encrypted--

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:46.490
send the configuration
to the production machine

00:14:46.490 --> 00:14:50.300
of Doodle front end along with
the credentials for Doodle

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:51.650
front end.

00:14:51.650 --> 00:14:56.450
And now Doodle front
end can authenticate

00:14:56.450 --> 00:15:00.207
to any of the other entities
that we described before.

00:15:00.207 --> 00:15:01.790
For example, Doodle
front end is going

00:15:01.790 --> 00:15:07.730
to authenticate to
another workload, which

00:15:07.730 --> 00:15:12.390
is going to be Doodle back end,
through, again, an ALTS, which

00:15:12.390 --> 00:15:14.190
we reauthenticate the channel.

00:15:14.190 --> 00:15:17.730
And from the Doodle
back end standpoint,

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:19.530
Doodle front end
is going to appear

00:15:19.530 --> 00:15:21.490
as the client-authenticated
entity.

00:15:28.180 --> 00:15:30.130
Another principle
that we have is

00:15:30.130 --> 00:15:34.690
to use channel-bound credentials
for authenticating services

00:15:34.690 --> 00:15:37.170
within the production network.

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:38.870
So what is a channel?

00:15:38.870 --> 00:15:42.140
You can see the channel
as a TLS session

00:15:42.140 --> 00:15:43.580
between a client and a server.

00:15:47.710 --> 00:15:50.010
And by channel-bound,
what I mean

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:53.520
is that the proof
of authentication

00:15:53.520 --> 00:15:57.510
is only relevant within the
context of this channel.

00:16:00.300 --> 00:16:05.400
So, authentication credentials
can be of two forms.

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:07.400
One can be certificate
and private keys,

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:09.515
and the other can
be bearer tokens.

00:16:09.515 --> 00:16:11.390
And you can see on the
web that bearer tokens

00:16:11.390 --> 00:16:13.010
are pretty popular.

00:16:13.010 --> 00:16:14.870
But we view strongly
that they are

00:16:14.870 --> 00:16:17.840
the wrong choice
for authenticating

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:19.560
within your infrastructure.

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:20.550
Why is that?

00:16:20.550 --> 00:16:23.510
It's that basically bearer
tokens, by their very nature,

00:16:23.510 --> 00:16:25.460
can be replayed.

00:16:25.460 --> 00:16:29.780
And that is a big issue here
because you have Doodle front

00:16:29.780 --> 00:16:33.310
end on one end wanting
to talk to Doodle back

00:16:33.310 --> 00:16:35.330
end on the other end.

00:16:35.330 --> 00:16:41.960
But that's not the only channel
of communication that exists.

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:43.670
Doodle front end
is going to talk

00:16:43.670 --> 00:16:46.770
to a bunch of different
other services,

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:50.450
such as the load reporting
service or the log service

00:16:50.450 --> 00:16:53.420
or the name resolution service.

00:16:53.420 --> 00:16:57.860
And, when that happens, you
don't want the load reporting

00:16:57.860 --> 00:17:00.050
service or any of
the services to be

00:17:00.050 --> 00:17:03.890
able to replay the bearer
token of Doodle front end

00:17:03.890 --> 00:17:05.079
to Doodle back end--

00:17:05.079 --> 00:17:11.030
in other terms, to impersonate
Doodle front end to the--

00:17:11.030 --> 00:17:14.420
sorry, to impersonate Doodle
front end to Doodle back end.

00:17:14.420 --> 00:17:18.109
And what that means
is that we really

00:17:18.109 --> 00:17:19.609
want channel-bound credentials.

00:17:19.609 --> 00:17:23.810
And so again, for in
Google production,

00:17:23.810 --> 00:17:26.119
we use ALTS, which
is certificate- and

00:17:26.119 --> 00:17:27.500
private-key-based.

00:17:27.500 --> 00:17:30.062
And that doesn't suffer
from this big drawback

00:17:30.062 --> 00:17:30.770
of bearer tokens.

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:39.570
Another principle
is that you have

00:17:39.570 --> 00:17:43.565
to be able to grant access
based on multiple identities.

00:17:43.565 --> 00:17:44.940
And that's not
something that you

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:50.200
can do with, again, policies
just at the layer 3 and layer

00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:50.700
4.

00:17:53.430 --> 00:17:57.540
Each service, each workload,
exposes methods and sometimes

00:17:57.540 --> 00:17:58.800
user data.

00:17:58.800 --> 00:18:02.730
And all this access might
be explicitly authorized

00:18:02.730 --> 00:18:05.260
by policies.

00:18:05.260 --> 00:18:08.840
Now let's talk about
the different identities

00:18:08.840 --> 00:18:13.550
that we have here on the
network on the request path.

00:18:13.550 --> 00:18:17.720
You first have the workload
identities or service identity

00:18:17.720 --> 00:18:19.790
or channel identities
or peer identities.

00:18:19.790 --> 00:18:23.160
All these things
are the same thing.

00:18:23.160 --> 00:18:26.690
And these are ingress, front
end, back end, health service.

00:18:26.690 --> 00:18:30.110
These are the identities that
I talked about previously.

00:18:30.110 --> 00:18:32.570
But at the beginning
of the request,

00:18:32.570 --> 00:18:37.730
you also have another identity,
which is the original requester

00:18:37.730 --> 00:18:38.780
identity.

00:18:38.780 --> 00:18:42.830
And that can be, for example,
Bob checking his Gmail

00:18:42.830 --> 00:18:44.900
on his phone.

00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:49.460
And it's very important for
this original requester identity

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:55.310
to be forwarded along the chain
from ingress to the database

00:18:55.310 --> 00:18:57.350
because, at the
end of the chain,

00:18:57.350 --> 00:19:01.190
it's Bob's data that we want
to retrieve from the database.

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:08.530
And let's see how this
can work hand-in-hand

00:19:08.530 --> 00:19:13.080
with the three identities.

00:19:13.080 --> 00:19:16.290
Along the chain,
each workload can

00:19:16.290 --> 00:19:20.160
decide which peer
identities are allowed

00:19:20.160 --> 00:19:24.510
to present such original
requester identities.

00:19:24.510 --> 00:19:28.230
And here, for example, from
the database point of view,

00:19:28.230 --> 00:19:31.020
the database can say,
hey, it's perfectly

00:19:31.020 --> 00:19:33.600
legitimate for the
back end to present me

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:37.710
with an original
requester identity.

00:19:37.710 --> 00:19:41.130
But with health service, which
is just there to make sure

00:19:41.130 --> 00:19:43.110
that I'm alive--

00:19:43.110 --> 00:19:46.560
this one is certainly not
privileged to present me

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:49.087
with an end user identity.

00:19:54.940 --> 00:19:57.840
The first principle-- which
is going to be the last here,

00:19:57.840 --> 00:20:01.170
and that's probably the
most important one--

00:20:01.170 --> 00:20:07.480
is that your platform must
implement security by default.

00:20:07.480 --> 00:20:11.080
And if you see
this diagram here,

00:20:11.080 --> 00:20:14.140
you have what is provided
by the platform, which

00:20:14.140 --> 00:20:17.830
is at the bottom, and
the application logic,

00:20:17.830 --> 00:20:20.520
which is at the top.

00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:27.490
The platform supplies service A
and service B with credentials,

00:20:27.490 --> 00:20:31.510
with an identity, automatically
without the application

00:20:31.510 --> 00:20:32.872
having to think about it--

00:20:32.872 --> 00:20:34.330
without the
application ever having

00:20:34.330 --> 00:20:36.160
to think about that,
you know, there's

00:20:36.160 --> 00:20:38.543
some magic key
distribution going on here.

00:20:38.543 --> 00:20:39.210
It just happens.

00:20:42.110 --> 00:20:48.240
When A wants to call the
get_info() method on B, again,

00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:51.000
just by virtue of using
the RPC framework,

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:53.520
automatically a
channel is created--

00:20:53.520 --> 00:20:56.880
a mutually authenticated
channel that

00:20:56.880 --> 00:21:02.970
will now say that the caller
is A service and the callee

00:21:02.970 --> 00:21:03.610
is B service.

00:21:06.590 --> 00:21:12.870
And the service orders are
going to be able to configure

00:21:12.870 --> 00:21:15.210
some authorization checks here.

00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:20.850
For example, service B can say,
identity A service can access

00:21:20.850 --> 00:21:22.590
B.get_info().

00:21:22.590 --> 00:21:25.900
And since this is the
case here, the call

00:21:25.900 --> 00:21:29.210
is going to be
able to go through.

00:21:29.210 --> 00:21:32.380
But again, all this happens
at the platform level

00:21:32.380 --> 00:21:38.290
without the involvement
of the application logic.

00:21:38.290 --> 00:21:41.780
And that's really important.

00:21:41.780 --> 00:21:46.460
Now, there may be some
types of authorization

00:21:46.460 --> 00:21:50.450
that really need some context
from the request itself.

00:21:50.450 --> 00:21:53.270
And such applications
are typically

00:21:53.270 --> 00:21:55.250
heavily multi-tenants.

00:21:55.250 --> 00:22:02.450
So if you want an example,
you can think of a file

00:22:02.450 --> 00:22:04.400
or directory service, right?

00:22:04.400 --> 00:22:07.160
Depending on the path
that is in the request,

00:22:07.160 --> 00:22:11.450
you will want to apply
one type of authorization

00:22:11.450 --> 00:22:13.580
versus another type
of authorization

00:22:13.580 --> 00:22:16.550
depending on the path.

00:22:16.550 --> 00:22:19.880
But even for these types
of application-driven

00:22:19.880 --> 00:22:24.530
authorization checks,
fine-grained access,

00:22:24.530 --> 00:22:26.900
we want to provide
frameworks for that.

00:22:26.900 --> 00:22:31.820
And internally, Google
IM is a good way--

00:22:31.820 --> 00:22:33.710
a good framework
for achieving this.

00:22:38.290 --> 00:22:41.820
So let's recap here
the five principles

00:22:41.820 --> 00:22:44.680
that we talked about.

00:22:44.680 --> 00:22:48.180
So the first one is protect
your network at the edge.

00:22:48.180 --> 00:22:50.800
Second one is to use
credentials-backed identities

00:22:50.800 --> 00:22:53.620
for all the entities
on the network.

00:22:53.620 --> 00:22:56.850
The third one is to use
channel-bound credentials

00:22:56.850 --> 00:22:59.070
for authenticating services.

00:22:59.070 --> 00:23:03.720
Four, to grant access based
on multiple identities

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:04.790
if this is applicable.

00:23:04.790 --> 00:23:06.540
You don't always have
multiple identities.

00:23:06.540 --> 00:23:09.930
But if you do, you need to
grant access based on that.

00:23:09.930 --> 00:23:12.780
And the fifth one, and the
most important one again,

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:17.060
is that the platform
implements security by default.

00:23:17.060 --> 00:23:18.450
And I cannot stress that enough.

00:23:18.450 --> 00:23:20.840
If there's one thing that
I want you to take away

00:23:20.840 --> 00:23:24.980
from this talk, is the role
of the platform for security.

00:23:24.980 --> 00:23:28.460
Like, don't let each team
of in your organization

00:23:28.460 --> 00:23:29.630
reinvent the wheel--

00:23:29.630 --> 00:23:31.415
reinvent its own framework.

00:23:34.030 --> 00:23:38.200
Settle on one thing and
make it better over time.

00:23:38.200 --> 00:23:40.590
So, Istio security
has been informed

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:42.570
by all these principles.

00:23:42.570 --> 00:23:47.430
And now Samrat will tell
you how these principles

00:23:47.430 --> 00:23:49.200
are being implemented in Istio.

00:23:49.200 --> 00:23:52.190
SAMRAT RAY: Thank you, Julien.

00:23:52.190 --> 00:23:56.540
So, Julien outlaid some
very important principles

00:23:56.540 --> 00:24:00.920
about how we at Google
protect our production

00:24:00.920 --> 00:24:04.100
environment against some fairly
sophisticated attacks, right?

00:24:04.100 --> 00:24:07.160
And increasingly, we find as
enterprises get more digitized

00:24:07.160 --> 00:24:10.287
and move to cloud
platforms, enterprises

00:24:10.287 --> 00:24:11.870
are exposed to similar
sort of threats

00:24:11.870 --> 00:24:13.910
that Google has been
exposed to over the years.

00:24:13.910 --> 00:24:16.310
And we feel that it's
increasingly irrelevant

00:24:16.310 --> 00:24:18.800
that enterprises adopt
such principles to protect

00:24:18.800 --> 00:24:20.610
their own production
environment.

00:24:20.610 --> 00:24:22.880
However, one of the big
challenges here is--

00:24:22.880 --> 00:24:25.550
I'm sure all of you realize
what Julien presented was

00:24:25.550 --> 00:24:27.590
technically complex, right?

00:24:27.590 --> 00:24:30.410
It's non-trivial to
do this in any shape

00:24:30.410 --> 00:24:33.140
or form in an enterprise
environment, right?

00:24:33.140 --> 00:24:36.200
And one of the
things immediately

00:24:36.200 --> 00:24:38.720
that strikes you when
Julien presents something

00:24:38.720 --> 00:24:42.050
like this was that, how does
this apply to my production

00:24:42.050 --> 00:24:42.830
environment?

00:24:42.830 --> 00:24:45.920
It doesn't look anything like
Google's production environment

00:24:45.920 --> 00:24:46.832
at all, right?

00:24:46.832 --> 00:24:49.040
The problem is not hundreds
of millions of endpoints.

00:24:49.040 --> 00:24:50.060
It's not scale.

00:24:50.060 --> 00:24:52.650
The problem is
heterogeneity, right?

00:24:52.650 --> 00:24:55.153
I have different workloads
from different generations

00:24:55.153 --> 00:24:57.320
using different protocols
using different languages,

00:24:57.320 --> 00:24:58.760
all sitting within
my data center.

00:24:58.760 --> 00:25:00.940
There is no uniform
deployment model, right?

00:25:00.940 --> 00:25:03.680
They're across different
kinds of run times.

00:25:03.680 --> 00:25:06.305
There is no standard application
framework like the kind Julian

00:25:06.305 --> 00:25:07.013
described, right?

00:25:07.013 --> 00:25:08.570
Because it's been
acquired over time

00:25:08.570 --> 00:25:12.750
across various third-party
software in many cases.

00:25:12.750 --> 00:25:16.580
So this new security approach,
which was described elegantly,

00:25:16.580 --> 00:25:18.320
is actually very
technically complex

00:25:18.320 --> 00:25:20.870
to roll out in my
environment, right?

00:25:20.870 --> 00:25:23.240
And lastly, even if I do
attempt to roll it out,

00:25:23.240 --> 00:25:24.740
how do I ensure the
success of this?

00:25:24.740 --> 00:25:25.740
How do I measure this?

00:25:25.740 --> 00:25:28.610
How do I get a visibility in
such a complex environment

00:25:28.610 --> 00:25:31.100
as how to achieve this?

00:25:31.100 --> 00:25:33.380
And as a product manager,
these are the kind

00:25:33.380 --> 00:25:37.450
of problems we landed
up thinking about as to,

00:25:37.450 --> 00:25:39.230
like, how do we
make what is really

00:25:39.230 --> 00:25:41.720
necessary from a security
perspective much more

00:25:41.720 --> 00:25:45.350
accessible to the
enterprise customer?

00:25:45.350 --> 00:25:48.057
And with that is how we started
thinking about this problem.

00:25:48.057 --> 00:25:49.640
We didn't start
thinking about saying,

00:25:49.640 --> 00:25:52.050
we want to build security
features for Istio.

00:25:52.050 --> 00:25:53.390
Actually, we weren't
even thinking about Istio

00:25:53.390 --> 00:25:54.973
when we started off
with this problem.

00:25:54.973 --> 00:25:57.800
We wanted to make these security
principles and this posture

00:25:57.800 --> 00:25:59.300
more approachable, right?

00:25:59.300 --> 00:26:01.970
And we started to think
about how we can do that.

00:26:01.970 --> 00:26:05.180
And it is over time and
looking at this problem

00:26:05.180 --> 00:26:08.030
in an incremental manner that we
realized that having something

00:26:08.030 --> 00:26:12.500
like Istio is very important
to enable enterprises to be

00:26:12.500 --> 00:26:15.370
able to adopt these principles.

00:26:15.370 --> 00:26:17.570
So, let's talk
about the primitives

00:26:17.570 --> 00:26:21.020
that build up to form Istio
and make this possible, right?

00:26:21.020 --> 00:26:23.000
The first thing
that you need to do,

00:26:23.000 --> 00:26:25.820
given your heterogeneous
infrastructure and the fact

00:26:25.820 --> 00:26:28.550
that you have all kinds of
protocols and languages,

00:26:28.550 --> 00:26:32.570
is the ability to virtualize
this application network

00:26:32.570 --> 00:26:33.830
endpoint, right?

00:26:33.830 --> 00:26:36.530
And Envoy as a
sidecar was something

00:26:36.530 --> 00:26:40.013
that our senior technical
leads did a long evaluation

00:26:40.013 --> 00:26:42.180
across a bunch of models,
and they came up and said,

00:26:42.180 --> 00:26:45.410
this is the most optimal way
of achieving this goal, right?

00:26:45.410 --> 00:26:47.720
And so, that gives
us this abstraction.

00:26:47.720 --> 00:26:51.440
In addition to-- so, a key
thing about that abstraction

00:26:51.440 --> 00:26:54.710
is now you can start being
transparent to your application

00:26:54.710 --> 00:26:58.100
code, because this now becomes
your policy enforcement point

00:26:58.100 --> 00:27:01.540
and as well as policy monitoring
and network monitoring

00:27:01.540 --> 00:27:02.173
point, right?

00:27:02.173 --> 00:27:03.590
So now you suddenly
have something

00:27:03.590 --> 00:27:05.132
that is the equivalent
of a NIC card,

00:27:05.132 --> 00:27:06.980
but at the application
level that gives you

00:27:06.980 --> 00:27:09.600
all these properties, right?

00:27:09.600 --> 00:27:12.470
Another key feature which
we did invest in in Envoy

00:27:12.470 --> 00:27:15.020
was to make sure that
the TCP support was

00:27:15.020 --> 00:27:17.360
more robust because this is
one of the key things that

00:27:17.360 --> 00:27:19.640
differentiates a homogeneous
infrastructure that's

00:27:19.640 --> 00:27:25.120
all RESTful to something that
is more enterprise-like, right?

00:27:25.120 --> 00:27:27.100
And so with this in
place, you can now

00:27:27.100 --> 00:27:30.190
have uniform policy enforcement
and you can have uniform access

00:27:30.190 --> 00:27:32.400
metrics and logs.

00:27:32.400 --> 00:27:35.190
The second question you might
ask is, I get the value Envoy.

00:27:35.190 --> 00:27:37.030
I get the value
of having a proxy.

00:27:37.030 --> 00:27:38.610
But why do I need
a service mesh?

00:27:38.610 --> 00:27:39.960
What is the service mesh?

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:42.360
I know I have a mess in
my production environment.

00:27:42.360 --> 00:27:45.580
Do I really need a
mesh on top of that?

00:27:45.580 --> 00:27:49.080
So it's-- yeah, the name "mesh"
a bit of a marketing play,

00:27:49.080 --> 00:27:50.190
right?

00:27:50.190 --> 00:27:52.020
What it really is is
a way to virtualize

00:27:52.020 --> 00:27:54.760
your network at the application
level to some extent, right?

00:27:54.760 --> 00:27:56.790
And in order to do
that, you actually

00:27:56.790 --> 00:27:59.790
need, just like with any
other networking software,

00:27:59.790 --> 00:28:01.278
or you need a
control plane, right?

00:28:01.278 --> 00:28:02.820
And you need a
control plane that can

00:28:02.820 --> 00:28:04.520
scale to thousands of services.

00:28:04.520 --> 00:28:07.080
And just like any other
networking system,

00:28:07.080 --> 00:28:09.500
you need to have a
central policy control

00:28:09.500 --> 00:28:11.160
or central policy visibility.

00:28:11.160 --> 00:28:12.900
And you need a way
to have a notion

00:28:12.900 --> 00:28:15.480
of being able to reason about
who's talking to whom, right?

00:28:15.480 --> 00:28:18.420
And you're able to do that at
the L3 level with your tools.

00:28:18.420 --> 00:28:20.850
And you must be able to do
that at this level, right?

00:28:20.850 --> 00:28:22.460
And how do you achieve that?

00:28:22.460 --> 00:28:25.050
The proxies by themselves
are not enough, right?

00:28:25.050 --> 00:28:27.870
And Istio really solves
for those problems.

00:28:27.870 --> 00:28:30.472
So we chose Istio not
because it's a cool thing,

00:28:30.472 --> 00:28:32.430
but because it actually
solves the real problem

00:28:32.430 --> 00:28:33.947
from a security perspective.

00:28:36.570 --> 00:28:39.230
So going forward, Istio does
more than just security,

00:28:39.230 --> 00:28:42.130
obviously, for those who
are familiar with it.

00:28:42.130 --> 00:28:44.410
It does traffic management
like load balancing,

00:28:44.410 --> 00:28:48.880
as well as things like
ability to route with layer

00:28:48.880 --> 00:28:50.410
7 semantics.

00:28:50.410 --> 00:28:53.440
Of course, it does security, and
we'll deep dive into that today

00:28:53.440 --> 00:28:54.560
a little bit more.

00:28:54.560 --> 00:28:57.010
And it does telemetry,
which allows

00:28:57.010 --> 00:28:59.200
you to get various kinds
of signals on performance

00:28:59.200 --> 00:29:01.325
and error rates to make
sure that your services are

00:29:01.325 --> 00:29:04.642
more reliable and performant.

00:29:04.642 --> 00:29:07.460
So we'll go back and look
at those five principles

00:29:07.460 --> 00:29:11.270
and how you can apply them
using the technologies that we

00:29:11.270 --> 00:29:13.530
provide on Google
Cloud into Istio.

00:29:13.530 --> 00:29:15.650
So the first is about
protecting your VPC.

00:29:15.650 --> 00:29:18.080
In the context of cloud,
your production environment

00:29:18.080 --> 00:29:20.100
can be represented
as a VPC, right?

00:29:20.100 --> 00:29:22.390
And how do you protect
that at the edge?

00:29:22.390 --> 00:29:24.140
There are two main
things to achieve here.

00:29:24.140 --> 00:29:25.230
There are two things to do here.

00:29:25.230 --> 00:29:27.530
One is to protect your
public-facing endpoints, right,

00:29:27.530 --> 00:29:29.840
and protect them against
internet-based threats.

00:29:29.840 --> 00:29:31.810
So we provide a couple
of technologies there

00:29:31.810 --> 00:29:32.810
which are very powerful.

00:29:32.810 --> 00:29:33.920
One is Cloud Armor.

00:29:33.920 --> 00:29:35.690
Julien talked about
DDoS protection.

00:29:35.690 --> 00:29:37.310
Cloud Armor allows you to--

00:29:37.310 --> 00:29:41.080
it's sort of a sophisticated
WAF, which also has DDoS

00:29:41.080 --> 00:29:42.980
protection baked in, right?

00:29:42.980 --> 00:29:47.520
So this is a critical piece
of your front-end strategy.

00:29:47.520 --> 00:29:48.980
The second is--

00:29:48.980 --> 00:29:51.350
Julien talked a bit about
how we have an admin proxy

00:29:51.350 --> 00:29:54.260
to allow BeyondCorp-like
principles in order

00:29:54.260 --> 00:29:56.840
to grant access for our
employees to gain access

00:29:56.840 --> 00:29:57.915
to production systems.

00:29:57.915 --> 00:30:00.290
We have built a corresponding
thing called Identity-Aware

00:30:00.290 --> 00:30:03.290
Proxy, which allows you such--

00:30:03.290 --> 00:30:07.310
it's a good way to allow
humans and to secure

00:30:07.310 --> 00:30:10.313
human-based access to the
production environment.

00:30:13.692 --> 00:30:16.150
Having protected the front end,
you think of the perimeter.

00:30:16.150 --> 00:30:17.380
Actually, the
perimeter is not just

00:30:17.380 --> 00:30:18.505
what's facing the internet.

00:30:18.505 --> 00:30:21.093
In many cases, your perimeter
may be a departmental boundary

00:30:21.093 --> 00:30:23.260
that you wish to draw within
your network sometimes.

00:30:23.260 --> 00:30:26.350
Or in Kubernetes landscape,
we call those clusters

00:30:26.350 --> 00:30:27.280
or namespaces, right?

00:30:27.280 --> 00:30:29.380
In your other environments,
you might call it

00:30:29.380 --> 00:30:31.240
a network segment.

00:30:31.240 --> 00:30:34.420
You need to protect that against
other parts of your network

00:30:34.420 --> 00:30:37.910
that don't have the same
level of security sensitivity.

00:30:37.910 --> 00:30:40.210
So this internal
segmentation is also needed.

00:30:40.210 --> 00:30:42.550
And it still ingresses
a solution for that

00:30:42.550 --> 00:30:46.120
because it can act both as a
layer 4 as well as a layer 7,

00:30:46.120 --> 00:30:48.400
sort of a proxy in
order to segment

00:30:48.400 --> 00:30:52.730
two different pieces
of your infrastructure.

00:30:52.730 --> 00:30:55.840
And then, once you have
this front end protected,

00:30:55.840 --> 00:30:58.000
you need to now seal out
the perimeter, right?

00:30:58.000 --> 00:31:00.730
And that means defending
against exfiltration vectors

00:31:00.730 --> 00:31:01.790
and control.

00:31:01.790 --> 00:31:04.600
So one of the things we provide
is our firewall policies.

00:31:04.600 --> 00:31:08.050
Like, these are layer 3
policies to understand

00:31:08.050 --> 00:31:11.140
the out-- we need to be able to
control outbound communication

00:31:11.140 --> 00:31:12.580
from within your network.

00:31:12.580 --> 00:31:15.190
And now, with what
is just GA, is

00:31:15.190 --> 00:31:17.260
VPC Service Controls,
which is a way

00:31:17.260 --> 00:31:19.860
to extend this notion
of a secure perimeter

00:31:19.860 --> 00:31:22.765
to multi-tenancy cloud
services like GCS and BigQuery

00:31:22.765 --> 00:31:24.640
so that you can implement
a similar perimeter

00:31:24.640 --> 00:31:28.413
strategy for those as well.

00:31:28.413 --> 00:31:30.330
So, this is a diagrammatic
view of what I just

00:31:30.330 --> 00:31:31.590
talked about, all right?

00:31:31.590 --> 00:31:33.632
So as you can see here,
from a corporate network,

00:31:33.632 --> 00:31:35.632
you can gain access to
the Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:31:35.632 --> 00:31:36.990
You have Cloud Armor in front.

00:31:36.990 --> 00:31:39.740
And these are actually
implemented on Google Cloud's

00:31:39.740 --> 00:31:40.650
load balancer.

00:31:40.650 --> 00:31:42.310
So they are effectively
policy engine--

00:31:42.310 --> 00:31:44.130
both Identity-Aware
Proxy and Cloud Armor

00:31:44.130 --> 00:31:47.230
on Google Cloud load
balancer, right?

00:31:47.230 --> 00:31:50.730
Additionally, you can now
implement an Istio ingress

00:31:50.730 --> 00:31:52.470
that sits right
behind it to provide

00:31:52.470 --> 00:31:56.120
additional segmentation.

00:31:56.120 --> 00:31:57.620
So, let's take the
second principle,

00:31:57.620 --> 00:31:59.360
using credentials-backed
identities

00:31:59.360 --> 00:32:01.470
for all entities
on your network.

00:32:01.470 --> 00:32:03.650
Now, Julien talked
about having ALTS

00:32:03.650 --> 00:32:05.890
as being the source
of truth to provide,

00:32:05.890 --> 00:32:08.030
and the certificate manager
to provide identities

00:32:08.030 --> 00:32:11.780
to everything-- administrators,
hosts, and workloads.

00:32:11.780 --> 00:32:13.940
In your enterprise,
typically it's

00:32:13.940 --> 00:32:16.730
very hard to have this kind of
a uniform way of meaning trust.

00:32:16.730 --> 00:32:20.300
It's that assigning trust
to users and to hosts

00:32:20.300 --> 00:32:23.190
is typically a solved problem
in most production environments.

00:32:23.190 --> 00:32:25.310
You have doing this
for many years, right?

00:32:25.310 --> 00:32:27.800
What is really unsolved
as a problem is a way

00:32:27.800 --> 00:32:32.570
to assign identities in a
secure manner to the workloads.

00:32:32.570 --> 00:32:34.337
So this is what Istio
is trying to solve.

00:32:34.337 --> 00:32:36.170
It tries to complement
what you already have

00:32:36.170 --> 00:32:39.710
and add to that by providing
a certificate of authority

00:32:39.710 --> 00:32:42.140
for workloads, which is
different from necessarily

00:32:42.140 --> 00:32:44.630
the roots of trust that you
might use to, say, identify

00:32:44.630 --> 00:32:46.610
your users, which might
be an Active Directory,

00:32:46.610 --> 00:32:49.670
or identify your host, which
in this particular example,

00:32:49.670 --> 00:32:51.830
that could be the
Kubernetes master, which

00:32:51.830 --> 00:32:55.610
is able to determine the
trustworthiness of a node

00:32:55.610 --> 00:32:57.038
on which it is deployed.

00:32:57.038 --> 00:32:59.330
So, in this particular example,
taking the same example

00:32:59.330 --> 00:33:01.550
that Julien gave but now
applying it to Kubernetes,

00:33:01.550 --> 00:33:03.883
you have Alice, who is on a
corporate workstation trying

00:33:03.883 --> 00:33:07.490
to deploy a pod to a
Kubernetes cluster, right?

00:33:07.490 --> 00:33:12.950
In this particular case, Alice
authenticates to the master

00:33:12.950 --> 00:33:15.110
and perhaps as a
particular namespace

00:33:15.110 --> 00:33:18.090
admin with deployment privileges
in order to be able to deploy.

00:33:18.090 --> 00:33:20.960
So therefore, we know she has
the right privileges in order

00:33:20.960 --> 00:33:24.440
to start the Doodle dot
front end over here, right?

00:33:24.440 --> 00:33:26.690
At this point, once
she is authenticated,

00:33:26.690 --> 00:33:30.820
the master can then deploy
the Doodle front end.

00:33:30.820 --> 00:33:32.900
And it assigns an
identity which we

00:33:32.900 --> 00:33:34.458
call a service account, right?

00:33:34.458 --> 00:33:36.750
So service accounts are now
becoming a common parlance.

00:33:36.750 --> 00:33:39.170
It's used in Google
Cloud Platform, in AWS,

00:33:39.170 --> 00:33:40.470
in Kubernetes.

00:33:40.470 --> 00:33:43.280
They basically represent an
identity that is typically

00:33:43.280 --> 00:33:46.180
associated with the workload.

00:33:46.180 --> 00:33:49.540
So at this point, once it's
deployed, if this workload were

00:33:49.540 --> 00:33:53.820
to be part of an
Istio-enabled environment,

00:33:53.820 --> 00:33:56.710
then there is a node
manager which is running on,

00:33:56.710 --> 00:33:58.690
that particular
node that then is

00:33:58.690 --> 00:34:02.350
able to use this identity that
was just issued by the master

00:34:02.350 --> 00:34:05.260
and create a CSR, or
a certificate request,

00:34:05.260 --> 00:34:09.159
to the service mesh
certificate authority, right?

00:34:09.159 --> 00:34:12.070
And the service mesh certificate
authority now takes this

00:34:12.070 --> 00:34:17.350
identity and encodes it into a
X.509 credential that is then

00:34:17.350 --> 00:34:18.850
placed--

00:34:18.850 --> 00:34:23.120
that is provided to Doodle
front end for authentication.

00:34:23.120 --> 00:34:26.610
Traditional X.509 certificate
authenticated hosts.

00:34:26.610 --> 00:34:30.230
So we worked with the
open source community

00:34:30.230 --> 00:34:32.847
to come up with a way
that everybody agrees on--

00:34:32.847 --> 00:34:34.639
at least within the
open source community--

00:34:34.639 --> 00:34:38.850
of encoding a workload identity
into an X.509 certificate.

00:34:38.850 --> 00:34:41.159
This is called SPIFFE.

00:34:41.159 --> 00:34:43.880
And the format that
we all agreed upon

00:34:43.880 --> 00:34:46.429
was how it would be
encoded in X.509.

00:34:46.429 --> 00:34:48.840
And Istio supports that
and is able to interplay

00:34:48.840 --> 00:34:52.219
with any other
party that can also

00:34:52.219 --> 00:34:54.507
support the SPIFFE
format for certificates.

00:34:57.250 --> 00:35:00.010
Going to the third principle,
using channel-bound credentials

00:35:00.010 --> 00:35:02.590
for authenticating services.

00:35:02.590 --> 00:35:04.160
Julien touched
upon this lightly.

00:35:04.160 --> 00:35:09.070
I will give a concrete example
to make this real, right?

00:35:09.070 --> 00:35:11.890
You can, with Istio,
specify a policy

00:35:11.890 --> 00:35:16.690
that says, all communications to
service B must be authenticated

00:35:16.690 --> 00:35:18.160
over mutual TLS, right?

00:35:18.160 --> 00:35:20.200
As in that when we talked
about channel-bound--

00:35:20.200 --> 00:35:22.330
I'll cover that one more time--

00:35:22.330 --> 00:35:25.240
the identity actually must be
a certificate on the channel,

00:35:25.240 --> 00:35:27.990
right, such that identity
cannot be replayed.

00:35:27.990 --> 00:35:30.700
So in this particular example,
assuming the shared service,

00:35:30.700 --> 00:35:33.230
the logging service
was compromised,

00:35:33.230 --> 00:35:36.850
if we are only allowing
mutual TLS-based identities

00:35:36.850 --> 00:35:41.710
for authentication, then, even
if service A were to present

00:35:41.710 --> 00:35:45.190
its certificate to
the logging service,

00:35:45.190 --> 00:35:48.250
the certificate by itself cannot
be replayed because the logging

00:35:48.250 --> 00:35:50.160
service cannot
create a channel--

00:35:50.160 --> 00:35:51.815
right, a TLS channel--

00:35:51.815 --> 00:35:54.190
with the private keys that
correspond to the certificate,

00:35:54.190 --> 00:35:55.130
right?

00:35:55.130 --> 00:35:57.160
So this is a very key
principle in order

00:35:57.160 --> 00:35:59.950
for us to mitigate risk and
escalation of privileges

00:35:59.950 --> 00:36:02.620
associated with
impersonation, right?

00:36:02.620 --> 00:36:06.430
So that's that all peers-- once
you enable this mutual TLS,

00:36:06.430 --> 00:36:08.770
we can guarantee that all
peers are authenticated

00:36:08.770 --> 00:36:11.590
using certificates based
on SPIFFE credentials that

00:36:11.590 --> 00:36:13.890
are channel-bound.

00:36:13.890 --> 00:36:16.560
However, this is easier said
than done for your Brownfield

00:36:16.560 --> 00:36:17.780
applications, right?

00:36:17.780 --> 00:36:21.440
I imagine a world where you
have an existing service.

00:36:21.440 --> 00:36:22.290
You're done on mTLS.

00:36:22.290 --> 00:36:23.340
Voila.

00:36:23.340 --> 00:36:25.320
Here are some bunch
of phones ringing

00:36:25.320 --> 00:36:29.050
because a whole lot of clients
which you didn't know existed

00:36:29.050 --> 00:36:32.250
want to enable for mTLS
and they just broke, right?

00:36:32.250 --> 00:36:34.550
So it's very hard to
actually do this in practice.

00:36:34.550 --> 00:36:36.050
So one of the things
we did in Istio

00:36:36.050 --> 00:36:38.760
was to create this mode
called permissive mode.

00:36:38.760 --> 00:36:41.430
In the permissive mode, when
you set it to permissive mode,

00:36:41.430 --> 00:36:44.160
a server can accept both
mutual-TLS-authenticated

00:36:44.160 --> 00:36:46.200
clients as well as regular--

00:36:46.200 --> 00:36:47.540
your existing clients.

00:36:47.540 --> 00:36:49.950
So that's what we would
recommend for your Brownfield

00:36:49.950 --> 00:36:51.768
applications, is you
set all your services

00:36:51.768 --> 00:36:53.310
to this permissive
mode, which is now

00:36:53.310 --> 00:36:55.110
the default mode for Istio.

00:36:55.110 --> 00:36:58.380
And then you ensure
that all your clients

00:36:58.380 --> 00:36:59.280
are talking in mTLS.

00:36:59.280 --> 00:37:01.260
At some point, you
are able to determine

00:37:01.260 --> 00:37:03.180
that all clients
talking to a service

00:37:03.180 --> 00:37:04.738
are now mutual TLS enabled.

00:37:04.738 --> 00:37:06.780
And, at that point, you
can lock the service down

00:37:06.780 --> 00:37:08.030
by tuning it to a strict mode.

00:37:11.330 --> 00:37:15.020
We talked about the identity
of origin earlier, right?

00:37:15.020 --> 00:37:17.180
And we must be able
to propagate that

00:37:17.180 --> 00:37:19.700
as a forwardable
credential, right?

00:37:19.700 --> 00:37:22.880
So in this particular,
in the cloud environment,

00:37:22.880 --> 00:37:24.470
for example, you
can use identity

00:37:24.470 --> 00:37:27.290
of a proxy to authenticate
any human coming

00:37:27.290 --> 00:37:28.530
from a portable device.

00:37:28.530 --> 00:37:30.440
You can use certain
principles like there's

00:37:30.440 --> 00:37:32.660
device type, et cetera,
and authenticating

00:37:32.660 --> 00:37:34.520
to Identity-Aware Proxy, right?

00:37:34.520 --> 00:37:37.550
So those checks can be done
in order to grant access.

00:37:37.550 --> 00:37:39.950
Now, all this context that
is part of the client context

00:37:39.950 --> 00:37:40.790
can--

00:37:40.790 --> 00:37:42.330
what Identity-Aware
Proxy does is

00:37:42.330 --> 00:37:45.150
it encodes it in a token,
which we call RC token.

00:37:45.150 --> 00:37:48.290
It's a form of a
JSON Web Token that

00:37:48.290 --> 00:37:50.750
has been structured
specifically with great--

00:37:50.750 --> 00:37:52.070
we provide greater structure.

00:37:52.070 --> 00:37:54.320
And very importantly,
it's a short-lived token.

00:37:54.320 --> 00:37:57.290
This reduces insider risk
because if the token is logged

00:37:57.290 --> 00:37:59.850
or stolen, there is a sig--

00:37:59.850 --> 00:38:03.020
we are reducing the risk by
reducing the time for which it

00:38:03.020 --> 00:38:04.880
is valid, right?

00:38:04.880 --> 00:38:07.060
Where Istio adds
value in this process

00:38:07.060 --> 00:38:08.780
is being able to
authenticate-- or sorry,

00:38:08.780 --> 00:38:12.700
be able to validate that this
token is actually a valid token

00:38:12.700 --> 00:38:15.520
by contacting the issuer and
validating the fact that it's

00:38:15.520 --> 00:38:19.120
a valid token such that the
back end can now safely use

00:38:19.120 --> 00:38:21.400
the claims in order
to grant access

00:38:21.400 --> 00:38:24.240
or influence business
behavior, right?

00:38:24.240 --> 00:38:25.930
And thus, as you
can see, Istio is

00:38:25.930 --> 00:38:27.430
taking care of a
lot of the plumbing

00:38:27.430 --> 00:38:30.922
that you would normally have to
do in these particular cases.

00:38:30.922 --> 00:38:32.380
Going back to the
fourth principle,

00:38:32.380 --> 00:38:36.190
which is granting access based
on multiple identities, right?

00:38:36.190 --> 00:38:38.505
The main reason to do this
is to protect user data.

00:38:38.505 --> 00:38:39.880
So, many of you
might be handling

00:38:39.880 --> 00:38:42.608
data of your
customers, and this is

00:38:42.608 --> 00:38:43.900
the reason you want to do this.

00:38:43.900 --> 00:38:44.990
Not for any other reason.

00:38:44.990 --> 00:38:46.990
It's not a traditional
network security posture.

00:38:46.990 --> 00:38:48.910
It's more about user
data-- protecting that

00:38:48.910 --> 00:38:51.558
through access control.

00:38:51.558 --> 00:38:54.100
Let's take this example where
there is a privileged insider--

00:38:54.100 --> 00:38:57.160
for example, a support
engineer-- who can access,

00:38:57.160 --> 00:39:00.520
based upon real business
requirements, your user data

00:39:00.520 --> 00:39:02.440
or your customer data, right?

00:39:02.440 --> 00:39:04.960
But what you really want
the support engineer to do

00:39:04.960 --> 00:39:08.325
is to only be able to access the
data via the case tool, where

00:39:08.325 --> 00:39:10.700
the case tool implements a
lot of your business policies,

00:39:10.700 --> 00:39:12.742
like, for example, is that
a valid support ticket

00:39:12.742 --> 00:39:14.087
against this, right?

00:39:14.087 --> 00:39:16.420
Is the support engineer
properly logged into the system?

00:39:16.420 --> 00:39:17.710
Are we logging all accesses?

00:39:17.710 --> 00:39:19.150
So on and so forth, right?

00:39:19.150 --> 00:39:20.930
If you're able to
bypass this tool,

00:39:20.930 --> 00:39:23.650
then you're able to bypass
all those checks, right?

00:39:23.650 --> 00:39:25.270
But at the same
time, the case tool

00:39:25.270 --> 00:39:27.640
might be a tool
that is delivered

00:39:27.640 --> 00:39:29.980
by a bunch of your
developers internally

00:39:29.980 --> 00:39:32.950
or a third-party software which
is deployed by somebody else.

00:39:32.950 --> 00:39:34.120
The people who deploy--

00:39:34.120 --> 00:39:36.430
who have the deployment
privileges in the case tool

00:39:36.430 --> 00:39:38.810
need not have
access to the data.

00:39:38.810 --> 00:39:40.850
Imagine a financial service is--

00:39:40.850 --> 00:39:42.230
or financial records.

00:39:42.230 --> 00:39:44.060
The person who
runs a job may not

00:39:44.060 --> 00:39:48.193
be an insider to get your
quarterly report, right?

00:39:48.193 --> 00:39:49.610
But they have the
admin privileges

00:39:49.610 --> 00:39:51.250
to deploy their job, right?

00:39:51.250 --> 00:39:53.000
Here, this is an
example in support

00:39:53.000 --> 00:39:57.020
that's another example
in the FR kind of world.

00:39:57.020 --> 00:40:00.200
So, which means there are
two things that must be true.

00:40:00.200 --> 00:40:01.870
The access must come
from the case tool,

00:40:01.870 --> 00:40:07.700
and the authenticated user
which it is acting on behalf of

00:40:07.700 --> 00:40:10.130
must be a support
engineer, right?

00:40:10.130 --> 00:40:12.620
These two things must be true
in order to grant access.

00:40:12.620 --> 00:40:15.500
Thus, you have to reason
about both of these identities

00:40:15.500 --> 00:40:18.770
in order to grant access such
that this privileged insider

00:40:18.770 --> 00:40:21.500
cannot create an unauthorized
client and simply access

00:40:21.500 --> 00:40:25.820
because only the user
was authorized or was

00:40:25.820 --> 00:40:26.600
on the policy.

00:40:26.600 --> 00:40:31.400
Similarly, the case tool cannot
identify itself and, in using

00:40:31.400 --> 00:40:33.740
its ambient authority, have
broad access to the data.

00:40:33.740 --> 00:40:37.310
Because that's a sure way of
increasing an insider risk,

00:40:37.310 --> 00:40:37.850
right?

00:40:37.850 --> 00:40:41.510
So therefore, it must be working
under the delegated authority

00:40:41.510 --> 00:40:45.020
of a customer support
engineer, right?

00:40:45.020 --> 00:40:46.820
And this is a very
key reason why

00:40:46.820 --> 00:40:49.850
we need to create yet another
policy engine for Istio

00:40:49.850 --> 00:40:52.190
and not simply rely on
existing network policy

00:40:52.190 --> 00:40:54.860
unions that exist in
Kubernetes and outside, right?

00:40:54.860 --> 00:40:56.090
This is not a solved problem.

00:40:56.090 --> 00:40:59.610
Now, it's a new policy
language that we are building.

00:40:59.610 --> 00:41:02.657
And we will continue to
work on this over time.

00:41:02.657 --> 00:41:05.240
But this is the primary use case
that we are targeting, right?

00:41:05.240 --> 00:41:06.590
There's multiple
channels to reach

00:41:06.590 --> 00:41:08.960
different users can access for
different use cases, right?

00:41:08.960 --> 00:41:10.377
And how do we make
it easy for you

00:41:10.377 --> 00:41:11.990
to be able to
build such policies

00:41:11.990 --> 00:41:14.480
and implement user
data protection

00:41:14.480 --> 00:41:18.100
within your production
environment.

00:41:18.100 --> 00:41:20.250
So, we talked a lot
about two identities

00:41:20.250 --> 00:41:21.210
and origin identities.

00:41:21.210 --> 00:41:23.970
And you might wonder, what about
non-HTTP service interactions

00:41:23.970 --> 00:41:26.310
like MySQL, for example.

00:41:26.310 --> 00:41:27.990
And the answer is, that's true.

00:41:27.990 --> 00:41:32.848
We cannot reason about the
identity at the SQL protocol

00:41:32.848 --> 00:41:34.890
level because today, Envoy
doesn't understand it.

00:41:34.890 --> 00:41:37.800
Hopefully over time, we want to
increase the number of layer 7

00:41:37.800 --> 00:41:39.240
protocols that we understand.

00:41:39.240 --> 00:41:41.910
But today, we're
limited to HTTP, GRPC

00:41:41.910 --> 00:41:46.350
as the primary protocols
that we support, right?

00:41:46.350 --> 00:41:49.140
So in such cases, yes,
we are not able to reason

00:41:49.140 --> 00:41:51.690
so strongly about who is
the user on behalf of which

00:41:51.690 --> 00:41:52.990
the call is coming from.

00:41:52.990 --> 00:41:59.070
But we can still constrain
from which any access can

00:41:59.070 --> 00:42:00.280
be presented from.

00:42:00.280 --> 00:42:02.912
So for example, even if you
have the MySQL credentials,

00:42:02.912 --> 00:42:05.370
you should not be able to access
the database from anywhere

00:42:05.370 --> 00:42:07.410
except through service A, right?

00:42:07.410 --> 00:42:09.910
And we can enforce
that property, right?

00:42:09.910 --> 00:42:10.960
So no matter what--

00:42:10.960 --> 00:42:12.900
like, if your MySQL
credentials are stolen

00:42:12.900 --> 00:42:15.358
or it was in a source control
system somewhere and somebody

00:42:15.358 --> 00:42:17.730
accessed it, they will
still not gain access

00:42:17.730 --> 00:42:19.480
because they have to
go through service A.

00:42:19.480 --> 00:42:20.855
And that is still
being enforced.

00:42:20.855 --> 00:42:24.257
So there's still a VIN of our
TCP communications outside

00:42:24.257 --> 00:42:26.340
of where we don't understand
the layer 7 protocol.

00:42:29.560 --> 00:42:32.590
The last part is platform
implements security by default.

00:42:32.590 --> 00:42:34.300
Throughout this talk,
you might realize

00:42:34.300 --> 00:42:37.870
that I've kept talking about how
Istio implements all of these.

00:42:37.870 --> 00:42:39.880
I've never said how
your application

00:42:39.880 --> 00:42:42.370
needs to change in order to
adopt any of this posture.

00:42:42.370 --> 00:42:44.890
All of this complex
technological paradigms

00:42:44.890 --> 00:42:47.170
are all expressed
as policies that

00:42:47.170 --> 00:42:49.810
get enforced at the
proxy level, right?

00:42:49.810 --> 00:42:52.270
So these include encryption
of data in transit.

00:42:52.270 --> 00:42:54.010
It includes the
ability to auto-rotate

00:42:54.010 --> 00:42:55.480
certain keys-- a
very hard problem,

00:42:55.480 --> 00:42:58.270
actually, to do this at
scale and do it properly,

00:42:58.270 --> 00:43:00.910
so to ensure that you have the
right cryptographic hygiene.

00:43:00.910 --> 00:43:02.720
How do you protect
user data access

00:43:02.720 --> 00:43:04.680
by using both identities, right?

00:43:04.680 --> 00:43:06.430
This has traditionally
been a hard problem

00:43:06.430 --> 00:43:08.290
to enforce consistently across.

00:43:08.290 --> 00:43:11.410
And how do you monitor
security posture consistently

00:43:11.410 --> 00:43:13.240
across a heterogeneous
environment, right?

00:43:13.240 --> 00:43:15.870
Now you have the ability
to do all of that

00:43:15.870 --> 00:43:19.510
because you are able to
use something like Istio.

00:43:19.510 --> 00:43:21.700
The last point deserves
a little more attention

00:43:21.700 --> 00:43:23.590
since I did not cover it before.

00:43:23.590 --> 00:43:25.350
And it's important
to understand that--

00:43:25.350 --> 00:43:27.880
and in a new world, let's
think of a MapReduce workload

00:43:27.880 --> 00:43:29.860
that comes up on a
bunch of containers,

00:43:29.860 --> 00:43:33.070
access your sensitive data as
a bad job, and then disappears.

00:43:33.070 --> 00:43:34.660
It does a bad job.

00:43:34.660 --> 00:43:37.785
You go back, look at your
network flow logs a week later,

00:43:37.785 --> 00:43:39.160
a month later,
and try to explain

00:43:39.160 --> 00:43:42.250
to internal and external
auditors what just happened.

00:43:42.250 --> 00:43:45.910
A bunch of IPs showed up,
contacted this back-end IP.

00:43:45.910 --> 00:43:48.010
And then a bunch of
IPs again showed up,

00:43:48.010 --> 00:43:50.052
and new IPs that
contacted something else.

00:43:50.052 --> 00:43:51.760
And we have no idea
what that IP actually

00:43:51.760 --> 00:43:54.052
meant because it's a completely
different workload that

00:43:54.052 --> 00:43:55.930
ran in different
points in time, right?

00:43:55.930 --> 00:43:57.820
Now you can actually
have logs that has

00:43:57.820 --> 00:44:01.270
an identity for that workload,
not of the host, right?

00:44:01.270 --> 00:44:03.820
And you're now able to
have this visibility

00:44:03.820 --> 00:44:06.040
across a heterogeneous
set of environments,

00:44:06.040 --> 00:44:09.050
heterogeneous set of protocols,
including the TCP protocol

00:44:09.050 --> 00:44:10.080
which I just explained.

00:44:10.080 --> 00:44:12.490
Who accessed the MySQL
database is still

00:44:12.490 --> 00:44:15.100
expressible as an identity.

00:44:15.100 --> 00:44:16.593
So you're able to do that.

00:44:16.593 --> 00:44:18.010
So you're able to
understand that.

00:44:18.010 --> 00:44:21.490
And it makes your network
access logs much more meaningful

00:44:21.490 --> 00:44:22.780
in this new environment.

00:44:22.780 --> 00:44:25.750
And it helps you go through your
security and compliance audits

00:44:25.750 --> 00:44:28.480
much more easily, right?

00:44:28.480 --> 00:44:31.450
And you're able to do this in
a consistent manner independent

00:44:31.450 --> 00:44:34.090
of your infrastructure.

00:44:34.090 --> 00:44:38.460
So with that, I would
like to just explain

00:44:38.460 --> 00:44:40.320
how you can get started
if this is something

00:44:40.320 --> 00:44:41.550
that is of interest to you--

00:44:41.550 --> 00:44:43.080
how you can get engaged.

00:44:43.080 --> 00:44:45.060
To start with, I would
strongly recommend

00:44:45.060 --> 00:44:48.173
that you read our
infrastructure security papers,

00:44:48.173 --> 00:44:49.590
and in particular,
the application

00:44:49.590 --> 00:44:51.300
transport security
papers so that you

00:44:51.300 --> 00:44:53.820
have a deeper understanding
of how this actually works

00:44:53.820 --> 00:44:55.620
at a deep technical level.

00:44:55.620 --> 00:44:58.650
And then to engage in Istio, you
can start by reading the blogs

00:44:58.650 --> 00:45:00.360
and going to our
website, which has

00:45:00.360 --> 00:45:02.660
the concepts, a bunch of
examples, and how to get

00:45:02.660 --> 00:45:04.350
started in open source.

00:45:04.350 --> 00:45:06.180
On Google Cloud,
we have an Istio

00:45:06.180 --> 00:45:09.960
on GKE, which just sort
of makes it much more easy

00:45:09.960 --> 00:45:11.187
for you to just get started.

00:45:11.187 --> 00:45:13.020
So I strongly recommend
that as a way to get

00:45:13.020 --> 00:45:15.832
started on a hands-on basis.

00:45:15.832 --> 00:45:17.290
If you want to know
more about what

00:45:17.290 --> 00:45:20.340
we're doing as Google's
secret sauce around Istio,

00:45:20.340 --> 00:45:23.610
please reach out to me at
samratray@google.com, right?

00:45:23.610 --> 00:45:28.110
And I'm happy to
help work with you

00:45:28.110 --> 00:45:31.740
to give you more information
about what we are doing to make

00:45:31.740 --> 00:45:35.030
this much more magical for you.

00:45:35.030 --> 00:45:37.193
So, thank you all
for taking the time

00:45:37.193 --> 00:45:39.610
